      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Methadone is an effective treatment for opioid dependency and chronic pain. A methadone derivative, levacetylmethadol, was withdrawn from the European market after being associated with torsade de pointes. To date, no association between methadone and this arrhythmia has been described."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate a series of methadone-treated patients experiencing torsade de pointes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective case series."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Methadone maintenance treatment programs in the United States and a pain management center in Canada."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "17 methadone-treated patients who developed torsade de pointes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "Chart review for concomitant arrhythmia risk factors and quantification of corrected QT interval (QTc)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The mean daily methadone dose was 397 +/- 283 mg, and the mean QTc interval was 615 +/- 77 msec. Fourteen patients had a predisposing risk factor for arrhythmia. A cardiac defibrillator or pacemaker was placed in 14 patients; all 17 patients survived."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This series raises concern that very-high-dose methadone may be associated with torsade de pointes. Given the likely expansion of methadone treatment into primary care, further investigation of these findings is warranted."
        }
      ]},
